<file path="Docs/MigrationProjectPlan.txt">
Phase 1 (only): Create a clean cloned project + new Git repo (TrackerSQL) without touching the live one.
Steps:
1.	Close the solution in Visual Studio.
2.	Copy the whole folder: File Explorer: C:\SRC\ASP.net\TrackerDotNet  →  C:\SRC\ASP.net\TrackerSQL
3.	In the new folder remove the existing Git history: Open a terminal in C:\SRC\ASP.net\TrackerSQL rmdir /s /q .git
4.	Rename the solution (optional but clearer):
•	Rename TrackerDotNet.sln → TrackerSQL.sln
•	If the main project folder is TrackerDotNet and you want it renamed: a. In Explorer rename folder TrackerDotNet → TrackerSQL b. Open TrackerSQL.sln in a text editor, change occurrences of TrackerDotNet to TrackerSQL (only in Project(...) line paths, not NuGet cache paths). c. Open the .csproj and (optional) change: <AssemblyName>TrackerSQL</AssemblyName> <RootNamespace>TrackerSQL</RootNamespace> (You can skip namespace changes now to avoid mass edits.)
5.	Initialize new Git repo: cd C:\SRC\ASP.net\TrackerSQL git init git add . git commit -m "Initial import from TrackerDotNet"
6.	Create the new remote repo (GitHub UI) named TrackerSQL, then: git remote add origin https://github.com/YourOrg/TrackerSQL.git git push -u origin main (If your default branch is master replace main.)
7.	Open TrackerSQL.sln in Visual Studio; build to confirm it still compiles.
Important tradeoff (concise):
•	Pros: Zero risk of accidental changes leaking to production.
•	Cons: Every bugfix now must be manually applied twice (TrackerDotNet and TrackerSQL). You must institute a tiny checklist for that.
Stop here.
If you want the next phase (adding SQL connection + provider toggle in the new project) say “next”.










Migration Project Plan (Expanded Step‑By‑Step – Data First, Code Second)
Project: TrackerDotNet (Access) → TrackerSQL (SQL Server)
Framework: .NET Framework 4.8
Strategy: 1) Clone project & migrate schema/data first. 2) Validate data. 3) Only then change code (queries / DAL / UI).

===============================================================================
PHASE 0 – GOALS & CONSTRAINTS (UNCHANGED CORE OBJECTIVES)
===============================================================================
1. Replace Microsoft Access (OleDb, 32‑bit) with SQL Server / SQL Express.
2. Minimize UI breakage (WebForms + SqlDataSource / ObjectDataSource).
3. Centralize data access later (ITrackerDb / TrackerDbSql abstraction).
4. Support two connection strings (Security + Data) OR unify if host limits.
5. Remove Access SQL idioms (iif, IsNull, #dates#, ? parameters).
6. Enable 64‑bit hosting (remove Jet/Ace provider requirement).
7. Lay groundwork for future refactors (repositories, tests, stored procs).

Deliverables (Phase 0 Done When):
- This plan committed under /Docs.
- Access DB backup stored safely (read‑only).
- Decision logged: “Data-first / no code changes until data sign-off”.

===============================================================================
PHASE 1 – INVENTORY & AUDIT (NO CODE CHANGES)
===============================================================================
Objective: Capture full schema + usage footprint.

Action Steps:
1. Ensure you’re on latest stable: 
   git fetch origin
   git checkout version/2.1.3.0
   git pull
2. Create folders (if missing):
   PowerShell:
     New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path .\Docs\Generated -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
     New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path .\Docs\Reports   -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Out-Null
3. Backup Access file(s):
   Copy-Item .\App_Data\*.mdb .\Backups\ -Force
4. Produce schema & profile (Option A – existing ShowTableStruct.aspx if extended; else Option B below).

Option B – PowerShell schema export (create Tools\Export-AccessSchema.ps1):
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
param(
  [string]$AccessFile = ".\App_Data\TrackerData.mdb",
  [string]$OutJson = ".\Docs\Generated\AccessSchema.json",
  [string]$OutProfile = ".\Docs\Reports\AccessProfile.tsv"
)

Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Data
$cs = "Provider=Microsoft.ACE.OLEDB.12.0;Data Source=$AccessFile;Persist Security Info=False;"
$cn = New-Object System.Data.OleDb.OleDbConnection($cs); $cn.Open()

$tables = $cn.GetOleDbSchemaTable([System.Data.OleDb.OleDbSchemaGuid]::Tables, @($null,$null,$null,"TABLE"))
$result = @()
foreach ($row in $tables.Rows) {
  $tName = $row.TABLE_NAME
  $cols = $cn.GetOleDbSchemaTable([System.Data.OleDb.OleDbSchemaGuid]::Columns, @($null,$null,$tName,$null))
  $cObjs = @()
  foreach ($c in $cols.Rows) {
    $cObjs += [pscustomobject]@{
      ColumnName = $c.COLUMN_NAME
      Ordinal    = $c.ORDINAL_POSITION
      DataType   = $c.DATA_TYPE
      Width      = $c.CHARACTER_MAXIMUM_LENGTH
      Nullable   = ($c.IS_NULLABLE -eq "YES")
    }
  }
  $cmd = $cn.CreateCommand(); $cmd.CommandText = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM [$tName]"
  $count = $cmd.ExecuteScalar()
  $result += [pscustomobject]@{ Table=$tName; RowCount=$count; Columns=$cObjs }
}
$cn.Close()
$result | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 7 | Out-File $OutJson -Encoding UTF8
"Table`tColumn`tRowCount" | Out-File $OutProfile -Encoding UTF8
$result | ForEach-Object { $_.Columns | ForEach-Object { "$($_.Table)`t$($_.ColumnName)`t$($_.RowCount)" | Add-Content $OutProfile } }
Write-Host "Schema exported to $OutJson"
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Run export:
   powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\Tools\Export-AccessSchema.ps1
6. Search for Access idioms (create Docs\Reports\AccessIdiomHits_Initial.txt):
   Select-String -Path . -Pattern "iif\(|IsNull\(| Date\(\)| Now\(\)|#[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}#|\s\?[\s,)\r\n]" |
     Select Path,LineNumber,Line | Out-File .\Docs\Reports\AccessIdiomHits_Initial.txt
7. Manually list all <asp:SqlDataSource> occurrences:
   Select-String -Path . -Pattern "<asp:SqlDataSource" > .\Docs\Reports\SqlDataSources.txt
8. Create summary file: Docs\Reports\InventorySummary.md (tables count, largest tables, etc.)

Phase 1 Done When:
- AccessSchema.json + AccessProfile.tsv + InventorySummary.md committed
- SqlDataSources.txt & AccessIdiomHits_Initial.txt committed

===============================================================================
PHASE 2 – NEW BRANCH & PROJECT IDENTITY (DATA STILL FIRST)
===============================================================================
We keep original intact; create working area.

Steps:
1. Create maintenance branch (if needed):
   git checkout version/2.1.3.0
   git checkout -b version/2.x-access
   git push -u origin version/2.x-access
2. Create migration feature branch:
   git checkout version/2.1.3.0
   git checkout -b feature/sql-migration
3. Copy project to TrackerSQL (SIDE-BY-SIDE):
   Copy-Item -Recurse -Path .\TrackerDotNet -Destination .\TrackerSQL
4. Rename project file inside new folder (if necessary) to TrackerSQL.csproj.
5. Open solution, Add Existing Project -> TrackerSQL\TrackerSQL.csproj.
6. Edit TrackerSQL.csproj: change <AssemblyName> and <RootNamespace> to TrackerSQL.
7. Build both projects (no functional changes yet).
8. Commit changes:
   git add TrackerSQL
   git commit -m "Clone project to TrackerSQL (pre-migration)"

Phase 2 Done When: TrackerSQL builds successfully; no code logic altered.

===============================================================================
PHASE 3 – SCHEMA MIGRATION (DESIGN SQL SCHEMA – NO APP CODE CHANGE)
===============================================================================
Goal: Author SQL Server schema (Database\Schema.sql) from Access schema inventory.

Data Type Mapping Reference (apply consistently):
  AutoNumber -> INT IDENTITY(1,1)
  YES/NO -> BIT
  TEXT(n) -> NVARCHAR(n)
  MEMO -> NVARCHAR(MAX)
  CURRENCY -> DECIMAL(19,4)
  SINGLE -> REAL (or DECIMAL(10,4) if precision needed)
  DOUBLE -> FLOAT
  DATE/TIME -> DATETIME2(0) (or DATETIME2(3) if time precision needed)
  OLE Object -> VARBINARY(MAX)

Steps:
1. Create Database directory if missing: New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path .\Database -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
2. Draft Schema_Draft.sql from AccessSchema.json (include tables in dependency order; parent tables first).
3. Add explicit PRIMARY KEY constraints.
4. Add indexes for frequent filters: (SortOrder), (ItemDesc), (City), etc.
5. OPTIONAL: Add ROWVERSION columns (RowVer) for tables with frequent updates (defer usage until code phase).
6. Save final as Database\Schema.sql.
7. Create empty SQL databases:
   sqlcmd -S .\SQLEXPRESS -Q "IF DB_ID('TrackerData') IS NULL CREATE DATABASE TrackerData;"
   sqlcmd -S .\SQLEXPRESS -Q "IF DB_ID('TrackerSecurity') IS NULL CREATE DATABASE TrackerSecurity;"
8. Apply schema:
   sqlcmd -S .\SQLEXPRESS -d TrackerData -i .\Database\Schema.sql > .\Docs\Reports\SchemaApply.log
9. Inspect log for errors; fix & re-run until clean.
10. Commit:
    git add Database\Schema.sql Docs\Reports\SchemaApply.log
    git commit -m "Add SQL Server schema"

Phase 3 Done When: Schema.sql applied cleanly (no errors) & committed.

===============================================================================
PHASE 4 – DATA MIGRATION (REPEATABLE SCRIPT)
===============================================================================
Goal: Move ALL data Access -> SQL preserving identity & row counts; record validation.

Script: Database\DataMigration.ps1 (bulk copy KeepIdentity).

Outline (create file):
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
param(
  [string]$AccessFile = ".\App_Data\TrackerData.mdb",
  [string]$SqlServer = ".\SQLEXPRESS",
  [string]$Database = "TrackerData",
  [switch]$Clear
)

Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Data
$accessCs = "Provider=Microsoft.ACE.OLEDB.12.0;Data Source=$AccessFile;Persist Security Info=False;"
$sqlCs    = "Server=$SqlServer;Database=$Database;Integrated Security=SSPI;MultipleActiveResultSets=True;"

$tables = @(
  # Order parents first (adjust to real list)
  "ServiceTypesTbl",
  "ItemTypeTbl",
  "CityTbl",
  "PackagingTbl",
  "PriceLevelsTbl",
  "PaymentTermsTbl",
  "InvoiceTypeTbl",
  "RepairStatusesTbl"
  # add remaining tables...
)

$log = ".\Docs\Reports\MigrationRun_$(Get-Date -Format yyyyMMdd_HHmmss).log"
"Migration start $(Get-Date -Format u)" | Out-File $log

$cnA = New-Object System.Data.OleDb.OleDbConnection($accessCs); $cnA.Open()

foreach ($t in $tables) {
  Write-Host "Migrating $t"
  if ($Clear) { sqlcmd -S $SqlServer -d $Database -Q "DELETE FROM [$t]" | Out-Null }
  $cmd = $cnA.CreateCommand(); $cmd.CommandText = "SELECT * FROM [$t]"
  $da = New-Object System.Data.OleDb.OleDbDataAdapter($cmd)
  $dt = New-Object System.Data.DataTable
  [void]$da.Fill($dt)

  if ($dt.Rows.Count -gt 0) {
    $bulk = New-Object Data.SqlClient.SqlBulkCopy($sqlCs,[Data.SqlClient.SqlBulkCopyOptions]::KeepIdentity)
    $bulk.DestinationTableName = $t
    foreach ($c in $dt.Columns) { $null = $bulk.ColumnMappings.Add($c.ColumnName,$c.ColumnName) }
    $bulk.WriteToServer($dt)
  }

  $sqlCount = sqlcmd -S $SqlServer -d $Database -h -1 -W -Q "SET NOCOUNT ON; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM [$t];"
  "$t`tin:$($dt.Rows.Count)`tout:$sqlCount" | Add-Content $log
}

$cnA.Close()
"Migration end $(Get-Date -Format u)" | Add-Content $log
Write-Host "Finished. Log: $log"
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run baseline migration:
  powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\Database\DataMigration.ps1

Create validation markdown: Docs\Reports\MigrationValidation.md:
  For each line t in log → table | in | out | Match?

Phase 4 Done When:
- Log file exists & every table in == out (or discrepancy documented).
- MigrationValidation.md committed.

Tag baseline:
  git tag data-migration-baseline
  git push origin data-migration-baseline

===============================================================================
GATE – DATA PHASE SIGN-OFF (STOP – NO CODE CHANGES BEFORE THIS)
===============================================================================
All MUST be true before proceeding:
[ ] Schema.sql applied cleanly
[ ] DataMigration.ps1 executed successfully
[ ] All table counts match (or accepted deltas documented)
[ ] Validation report committed
[ ] Tag data-migration-baseline created

Create Docs\Reports\DataPhaseSignoff.md:
  Date / Signer / Notes

===============================================================================
PHASE 5 – CONNECTION STRATEGY (START CODE CHANGES AFTER SIGN-OFF)
===============================================================================
Goal: Introduce SQL connection strings (still minimal UI change).

Steps:
1. In TrackerSQL\Web.config add:
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
<connectionStrings>
  <add name="TrackerData"
       connectionString="Server=.\SQLEXPRESS;Database=TrackerData;Integrated Security=True;MultipleActiveResultSets=True;"
       providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
  <add name="TrackerSecurity"
       connectionString="Server=.\SQLEXPRESS;Database=TrackerSecurity;Integrated Security=True;MultipleActiveResultSets=True;"
       providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
</connectionStrings>
<appSettings>
  <add key="UseUnifiedDatabase" value="false" />
</appSettings>
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Build & ensure no runtime errors (still referencing Access until queries switched).
3. Commit.

Phase 5 Done When: Web.config contains SQL connection strings; app still runs (using old logic).

===============================================================================
PHASE 6 – DATA ACCESS ABSTRACTION (OPTIONAL EARLY / OR LATER)
===============================================================================
(If you delay abstraction, refactor after verifying pages against SQL.)

Steps (when chosen):
1. Add /DataAccess/ITrackerDb.cs & TrackerDbSql.cs (as per initial plan).
2. Add TrackerDbFactory (static) returning new TrackerDbSql(connStr).
3. Convert one low-risk DAL file (e.g., PersonsTbl) to prove pattern.
4. Regression test People tab.

Phase 6 Done When: At least one DAL uses abstraction, pattern documented.

===============================================================================
PHASE 7 – REFACTOR UI DATA SOURCES (PAGE-BY-PAGE)
===============================================================================
Order (recommended): Items → Cities → ServiceTypes → Packaging → InvoiceTypes → PaymentTerms → PriceLevels → RepairStatuses → Remaining.

For each page:
1. Replace Access expressions:
   iif(IsNull(X),0,X) → ISNULL(X,0)
   LIKE ? → LIKE @Search
2. Replace positional ? params with @Named.
3. Ensure code-behind sets Session["SearchItemContains"] = "%" + input + "%"; (if needed).
4. Test CRUD (Insert/Edit/Delete) with SQL Server DB.
5. Log result in Docs\Reports\PageMigrationLog.md.

Phase 7 Done When: All targeted data sources converted & PageMigrationLog.md shows PASS entries.

===============================================================================
PHASE 8 – SQL SYNTAX ADJUSTMENTS (GLOBAL CLEANUP)
===============================================================================
Search & replace patterns:
- iif(  → IIF( or CASE WHEN
- IsNull( → ISNULL(
- Date() → CAST(GETDATE() AS date)
- Now()  → GETDATE()
- #yyyy-mm-dd# → 'yyyy-mm-dd'
- & concat → +
- TRUE/FALSE → 1/0
- ? params → @Named

Re-run idiom scan; produce AccessIdiomHits_Final.txt (must be empty or known safe).

Phase 8 Done When: Final scan clean; committed.

===============================================================================
PHASE 9 – CONCURRENCY & TRANSACTIONS (OPTIONAL ENHANCEMENT)
===============================================================================
1. Add ROWVERSION to high-contention tables (if not already).
2. Update UPDATE statements to include RowVer in WHERE.
3. Add transactional helper if multi-step operations exist.

Phase 9 Done When: Documented tables upgraded & tested (optional).

===============================================================================
PHASE 10 – TESTING STRATEGY IMPLEMENTATION
===============================================================================
Artifacts:
- Docs/Testing/SmokeChecklist.md
- Docs/Testing/CRUDChecklist.md

Smoke Checklist Includes:
- App loads
- Login (if applicable)
- Each Lookups tab loads
- Each CRUD operation works
- Search works (Items)

Execute & mark PASS/FAIL. Record results in TestRun_<date>.md.

Phase 10 Done When: All tests PASS or waivers approved.

===============================================================================
PHASE 11 – DEPLOYMENT PLAN
===============================================================================
Pre-Prod Rehearsal:
1. Run Schema.sql on staging SQL.
2. Run DataMigration.ps1 (fresh).
3. Deploy TrackerSQL site (staging IIS).
4. Point connectionStrings to staging DBs.
5. Run full smoke + CRUD test.
6. Produce Runbook: Docs\Runbook_Cutover.md (exact ordered steps).

Cutover Checklist:
- Freeze writes on old site.
- Final data migration (delta) if needed (or short downtime).
- Swap IIS binding or DNS to new site.
- Post-cutover smoke test (abbreviated).
- Monitor logs 1–2 hours.

Rollback Plan:
- Keep Access site & DB intact.
- If blocking issue: revert IIS binding to old site within X minutes.
- Document root cause before retry.

Phase 11 Done When: Runbook + rollback rehearsal completed; sign-off captured.

===============================================================================
PHASE 12 – CLEAN-UP
===============================================================================
Actions:
1. Remove OleDb references & any x86 build requirement.
2. Remove unused Access-only helper classes.
3. Update README with: prerequisites, Schema.sql, DataMigration.ps1, Web.config changes.
4. Tag release: tracker-sql-live.

Phase 12 Done When: No OleDb or ACE references, README updated, tag pushed.

===============================================================================
PHASE 13 – OPTIONAL ENHANCEMENTS
===============================================================================
1. Stored procedures for heavy writes.
2. MemoryCache for static lookups (10 min TTL).
3. Introduce Dapper for new DAL code.
4. Add /healthz endpoint checking DB connectivity.
5. Add integration tests (LocalDB) in CI pipeline.

